<%!-- English in every locale (see VutuvWeb.CompanyController), so the block
carries its own lang and nothing here goes through gettext. --%>
<div lang="en" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-6 py-6">
  <.card>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Investors</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      vutuv is an alternative to LinkedIn. The same job: a professional profile, a CV,
      the people you work with, the posts they write. Quieter manners, and a cost base
      that lets us keep them.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      If you want the deck or a conversation, write to
      <a
        href={"mailto:#{@facts_contact}"}
        class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300"
      >{@facts_contact}</a>.
    </p>
  </.card>

  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">The gated community</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      What people write on LinkedIn is not on the web. It is inside LinkedIn, and getting
      to it costs an account. Ask for a public profile without one and you get a page with
      the headline and a modal over the rest: "Sign in to view … full profile". Ask for
      the feed and you get a redirect to the sign-in form. Neither of those is a bug. It
      is the product: the content is the wall that makes people register.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      A member's professional record is therefore only worth what the platform lets it be
      worth. It cannot be linked to from a place that matters, cited in an article, read
      by the person hiring, or found later by someone who was never a member. Ten years of
      somebody's working life sit behind a door somebody else owns.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      On vutuv a profile is a web page. The whole profile, not a teaser: work history,
      qualifications, contacts, posts, all of it readable with no account, at a stable
      address the member can put on a business card. Every one of those pages also serves
      Markdown, plain text, JSON and XML for machines, an RSS feed for readers, and speaks
      ActivityPub, so somebody on Mastodon can follow a vutuv member without joining
      anything. Our own timeline sits behind a login like theirs, because a timeline is
      personal. The record does not.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      For an investor the point is not the principle. It is the acquisition cost. Every
      profile on vutuv is a public page that search engines index and language models
      read, so the network is its own front door.
    </p>
  </.card>

  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
      Why we can afford to be quiet
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      Nobody at LinkedIn wants it to be noisy. The apparatus does. LinkedIn's own newsroom
      reports
      <a
        href={@comparison.source}
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
        class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300"
      >{@comparison.employees} full-time employees</a>
      serving {@comparison.members} members. A payroll that size has to be earned back
      every quarter, and the only lever a social network has for that is attention. Hence
      the nudges, the notifications about nothing, the mail you did not ask for and the
      ranking that decides what you see.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      vutuv is built and operated by one person working with AI agents. Development,
      operations and moderation all sit in that one place, and the agents are the leverage
      that makes it possible. The stack does the other half: Elixir on the BEAM, a runtime
      built for hundreds of thousands of concurrent connections per machine, in front of
      PostgreSQL. Work that other stacks spread across a fleet fits on a handful of servers
      here, and it will keep fitting. We expect to need less hardware than the competition
      at every size we grow into.
    </p>
    <%!-- The link only where the ad system is switched on: `/ads` 404s while
          `:ads_enabled` is off, and a dead link on the page that explains the
          revenue model is the worst place to have one. The sentence stands
          either way, because the model is the model. --%>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      That is what buys the manners. The feed is strictly chronological, because no
      revenue depends on reordering it. There is no tracking, no profiling and nothing
      loaded from another host. The money comes from
      <.link
        :if={Vutuv.Ads.enabled?()}
        href={~p"/ads"}
        class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300"
      >one text ad per day</.link>
      <span :if={!Vutuv.Ads.enabled?()} class="font-semibold">one text ad per day</span>, the
      same ad for everybody, and that is enough because the bill it has to cover is small.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      An incumbent cannot copy this by deciding to be nicer. The pushiness is what pays
      for the apparatus, so dropping it means dismantling the apparatus first.
    </p>
  </.card>

  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Where we are</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      Read from the live database on every request, and published in the same figures at
      <.link href={~p"/system/nodeinfo/2.1"} class="underline">/system/nodeinfo/2.1</.link>.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <.figure_tile label="Members" value={en_count(@facts.members)} />
      <.figure_tile
        label="Active this month"
        value={en_count(@facts.active_month)}
        note="Signed in within 30 days"
      />
      <.figure_tile
        label="Active this half year"
        value={en_count(@facts.active_halfyear)}
      />
      <.figure_tile label="Posts" value={en_count(@facts.posts)} />
      <.figure_tile label="Replies" value={en_count(@facts.replies)} />
      <.figure_tile
        label="Fediverse accounts"
        value={en_count(@facts.fediverse_accounts)}
        note={"From #{en_count(@facts.fediverse_servers)} servers"}
      />
    </div>
  </.card>

  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Growth</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      The head count is recorded once a day, at 23:59 Berlin time.
    </p>
    <.growth_chart series={@series} />
    <p :if={@growth} class="mt-4 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      Over the {@growth.days} days from {Calendar.strftime(@growth.from, "%d %b %Y")} to
      {Calendar.strftime(@growth.to, "%d %b %Y")}, vutuv gained
      {en_count(@growth.members)} members and
      {en_count(@growth.fediverse_accounts)} Fediverse accounts.
    </p>
    <p :if={is_nil(@growth)} class="mt-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      This installation has not recorded two days yet, so there is no curve to draw.
    </p>
  </.card>

  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">How it spreads</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">It starts connected.</span>
        A new professional network normally opens in an empty room and has to fill it
        before it is worth joining. vutuv speaks ActivityPub from the first day, so its
        members are followable from the Fediverse and remote posts arrive here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Anyone may run it.</span>
        The software is MIT-licensed and self-hostable, on the internet or inside a company
        network. That is a distribution channel rather than a giveaway: every installation
        speaks the same protocol, so it enlarges the same network instead of forking it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </.card>

  <.card>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Talk to us</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      {@operator_name} operates this installation. Write to
      <a
        href={"mailto:#{@facts_contact}"}
        class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300"
      >{@facts_contact}</a>
      and you reach a person, not a form.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      Logos, screenshots and boilerplate for a story are on the
      <.link href={~p"/system/media-kit"} class="underline">media kit</.link>.
    </p>
  </.card>
</div>
